// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row fnparam_tuple_nested_sub_binds_nothing (tier 3). A NESTED
// tuple sub-pattern in a FUNCTION PARAMETER — `fn probe(((a, b), c): ((i64, i64),
// i64))` — is legal Rust and TODAY is refused "undefined variable 'a'": the
// fn-param tuple door's binder walk (sema_decl.cpp, the `p.has_key(la::NAMES)`
// branch) takes a name from a sub-node ONLY when it is a bare PAT_WILD and
// records "_" for every other kind, so a PAT_TUPLE element binds NOTHING and the
// body's first use of an inner binder is an undefined name. The grammar DOES
// produce the nested node (`pat_binding <- LPAREN pat_binding_list RPAREN =>
// PAT_TUPLE{NAMES}`), and the `let` door recurses into it, so this is the
// parameter position's own walk, not the grammar and not the tuple door in
// general. Same shape as match_tuple_door_nested_struct_binds_nothing (a
// container door with a whitelist of sub-pattern kinds) at a different door.
// The closure twin `|((a, b), c): ((i64, i64), i64)|` is refused identically.
// FOUND 2026-09-07v while writing counter-examples for the `mut`-modifier class
// (the modifier fix is orthogonal: this refuses with or without a `mut`).
// Rust: exit 0.
package fnparam_tuple_nested_sub_binds_nothing;
fn probe(((a, b), c): ((i64, i64), i64)) -> i32 {
    if a + b + c != 7i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
fn main() -> i32 {
    let t: ((i64, i64), i64) = ((1i64, 2i64), 4i64);
    return probe(t);
}
